---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Photo from '../components/Photo.astro';
import hoopHouse from '../assets/photos/hoop-house.jpg';
---
<Base
  title="The Farm: what we plan to grow | Windrose Farm"
  description="The orchard, gardens, apiary and vineyard planned for Windrose Farm, and where each one actually stands. So far, all four are plans."
>
  <div class="wrap page-head">
    <h1>What we plan to grow</h1>
    <p>
      None of this exists yet. These are the four things we intend to build,
      roughly in the order the land will allow, with where each one stands
      as of November 2026.
    </p>
  </div>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2>Orchard</h2>
      <p><span class="tag">Site chosen · nothing planted</span></p>
      <p>
        A protected orchard inside an elk-resistant enclosure, with fruit and
        nut trees chosen for northern Arizona's short season and alkaline
        soil. The plan mixes the trees with pollinator habitat,
        nitrogen-fixing plants and perennial groundcovers, so it works as a
        small ecosystem and not only as rows of fruit trees.
      </p>
      <p>
        The first plantings will be asparagus, jujube and cold-hardy apples.
        The site and the planting list are on <a href="/land/">The Land</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2>Gardens</h2>
      <p><span class="tag">Not started</span></p>
      <p>
        Vegetables, herbs, flowers and perennial crops, grown with compost,
        drip irrigation, mulch and companion planting. The main purpose is
        feeding the family while improving the soil.
      </p>
      <Photo
        class="inline"
        src={hoopHouse}
        alt="The bare hoop frame of a greenhouse standing in open ground, its plastic cover torn away"
      >
        The hoop house, abandoned by the previous owners and still standing.
        Bringing it back is part of the plan. With a short season, covered
        growing space adds weeks at both ends.
      </Photo>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2>Apiary</h2>
      <p><span class="tag">Not started · years away</span></p>
      <p>
        Bees come last. The plan is to bring in hives once the orchard and
        lavender are blooming and can help feed them. When that day comes,
        the goals are healthy colonies, pollination for the orchard and
        gardens, raw local honey and beeswax, and habitat for native
        pollinators as well.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is the part of the farm that comes most directly from
        <a href="/story/">Aunt Rosie</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2>Vineyard</h2>
      <p><span class="tag">Not scheduled</span></p>
      <p>
        A small vineyard, for learning and for small-batch winemaking. It
        isn't on the five-phase plan yet.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2>How the pieces fit</h2>
      <p>
        The four are meant to work as one system. Bees pollinate the orchard
        and the orchard feeds the bees. Compost feeds the gardens. Trees slow
        the wind, and better soil holds more water. That is the design, and
        whether it works here is what this site will record.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="next-step">
    <div class="wrap">
      <a class="btn" href="/seasons/">See the seasons →</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
